course: language
lesson: 54
title: Off with her head and the finest house on the river: Alice and Toad
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lewis Carroll and Kenneth Grahame both loved hyperbole and understatement, and they made jokes out of them. Carroll often takes a big saying and makes it really happen; Grahame gives us Toad, the greatest boaster on the river bank.
explain: When Alice is more than nine feet high and cries, she goes on «shedding gallons of tears, until there was a large pool all round her». People say «she cried buckets»; Carroll makes the buckets real, and later, when Alice has shrunk, she has to swim in her own tears.
explain: The Queen of Hearts shouts «Off with her head!» at everyone, but the Gryphon tells Alice: «It's all her fancy, that: they never executes nobody, you know». Her threats are hyperbole, like a cross big sister's.
explain: And Toad boasts about everything: his house is the «Finest house on the whole river», and in his song he is cleverer than all «The clever men at Oxford». The bigger the boast, the sillier he looks — and the more we laugh.
explain: Understatement can be kind, too: when the Mole tips the boat over, the Rat just laughs, «What's a little wet to a Water Rat?»
fact: Falling down the rabbit hole, Alice thinks «after such a fall as this, I shall think nothing of tumbling down stairs!» — a boast that she will now be very brave.
fact: Alice goes on «I wouldn't say anything about it, even if I fell off the top of the house!» and Carroll adds «(Which was very likely true.)» — a dark joke: after such a fall she wouldn't be able to say anything.
fact: The Queen of Hearts shouts «Off with his head!» or «Off with her head!» «about once in a minute», but the Gryphon says «they never executes nobody, you know»; Carroll is poking fun at people who shout big threats.
fact: The White Rabbit always panics about time: «Oh dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!» and «Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!»; he is a funny, exaggerated worrier.
fact: More than nine feet high, Alice cries «gallons of tears», making a pool «about four inches deep and reaching half down the hall»; Carroll turns the hyperbole of «crying buckets» into a real pool.
fact: At the Mad Tea-Party the Hatter says «It's always six o'clock now» and «it's always tea-time, and we've no time to wash the things between whiles»; Carroll makes «forever» come true as a joke.
fact: When the Mole politely praises Toad Hall, Toad cries «Finest house on the whole river», and then cannot help adding «Or anywhere else, for that matter».
fact: Toad's song boasts: «The clever men at Oxford / Know all that there is to be knowed. / But they none of them know one half as much / As intelligent Mr. Toad!» — he is not cleverer than the university teachers, and «knowed» is a deliberately wrong word for «known».
fact: The Rat tells the Mole there is «nothing — absolute nothing — half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats»; it is a loving exaggeration, the way people talk about what they love most.
fact: In prison Toad wails «This is the end of everything» and then «at least it is the end of the career of Toad, which is the same thing» — he makes the whole world about himself, and later he escapes.
fact: When the Mole grabs the sculls and tips the boat over, the Rat pulls him out laughing and says «What's a little wet to a Water Rat? I'm more in the water than out of it most days» — kind understatement that makes the Mole feel better.
exercise: Falling down the rabbit hole, Alice thinks «I shall think nothing of tumbling down stairs!». What is she doing? | Boasting that she'll be very brave now | Planning to throw herself down the stairs | Complaining that the stairs at home are too steep | Telling Dinah the cat to be careful on the stairs
exercise: Carroll adds «(Which was very likely true.)» after Alice says she wouldn't say anything if she fell off the house. What is the joke? | After such a fall she couldn't say anything at all | Alice is very good at keeping secrets | Alice lives in a very low house | Alice has fallen off the top of lots of houses before
exercise: What does the Queen of Hearts keep shouting? | «Off with her head!» | «Bring me my tarts!» | «Paint the roses red!» | «Let the game begin!»
exercise: What does the Gryphon tell Alice about the Queen's threats? | «they never executes nobody» | «she means every single word» | «she executes everyone at night» | «only the gardeners are in danger»
exercise: What is Carroll poking fun at with the Queen? | People who shout big threats | Queens who play croquet badly | Gardeners who paint roses | Children who are rude to queens
exercise: The White Rabbit says «Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!». What kind of character is he? | A funny, exaggerated worrier | A calm and patient rabbit | A rabbit in real danger | A rabbit who is never late for anything
exercise: Alice, more than nine feet high, sheds «gallons of tears». What happens? | They make a real pool round her | They dry up straight away | They fill the whole of Wonderland | They turn into a river of tea
exercise: Which English saying does the pool of tears make real? | «She cried buckets» | «She was over the moon» | «It's raining cats and dogs» | «She was down in the dumps»
exercise: Why is it always tea-time at the Mad Tea-Party? | It is always six o'clock, so it's tea-time forever | The Hatter loves tea far more than any food | The clocks in Wonderland have no hands | The March Hare stole all the other meals of the day long ago
exercise: Toad says Toad Hall is the «Finest house on the whole river». What is he doing? | Boasting | Measuring | Apologising | Joking about Rat
exercise: Toad sings that «The clever men at Oxford» don't know «one half as much / As intelligent Mr. Toad». Is it true? | No, it's a silly boast | Yes, Toad went to Oxford | Yes, Toad is a professor | No, Toad knows nothing
exercise: What does the Rat mean by «nothing — absolute nothing — half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats»? | He loves boats more than anything | It is a fact that scientists have proved | He thinks boats are a waste of time | He wants Mole to buy a boat from him
exercise: In prison Toad wails «This is the end of everything». What is really happening? | He is very upset and making it all about himself | The whole world is really about to come to an end | He is calmly telling the warders the truth | He is joking with the gaoler's daughter
exercise: When the boat tips over, the Rat says «What's a little wet to a Water Rat?». Why? | To play it down kindly so Mole feels better | Because he is very cross with the Mole | Because he has never been in the water | To show off that he can swim faster than Mole
exercise: How does the Rat feel about getting soaked? | He doesn't mind at all | He is furious | He is frightened | He is freezing and ill
test: «the queen chops off heads every minute, Wonderland is literally a horror film» What does the book really say? | The Gryphon says nobody is ever executed: her threats are hyperbole | She really does chop off a head every single minute of the day | The Queen only chops off the heads of the gardeners who are late for work | Alice is executed at the end of the trial
test: Your little brother reads that Alice made a pool of tears and tries to cry a pool in the bath. What should you tell him? | Carroll made a saying come true as a joke; real tears don't make pools | He needs to cry for longer to fill the bath | Only very tall people can cry pools of tears | He should drink lots more water first, so that he has enough tears to fill it
test: ★ «Toad says his house is the finest on the whole river, so it is, he lives there» What is right? | It's a boast; Toad always praises himself | It's a fact, because Toad knows his own house | It's sarcasm: Toad hates his house | It's understatement: Toad Hall is even finer
test: ★ «Rat says nothing is as good as messing about in boats, so school is pointless» What has gone wrong? | Rat was lovingly exaggerating about what he enjoys most | Rat is right, and boats are the only thing worth doing | Rat said that school is good, but that boats are even better | Rat was being sarcastic about boats
test: ★ Alice boasts she'd think nothing of tumbling down stairs. A cartoon shows her cheerfully jumping off a roof. What is wrong? | It misses Carroll's joke; nobody should ever try such falls | Nothing, Alice really does jump off the house | Alice does it, but in the book she uses a parachute | The cartoon should show her falling up instead
test: «Toad says it's the end of everything, so the book's over??» What really happens? | Toad is being dramatic; later he escapes | The world ends and the book stops | Toad stays in prison for ever and ever after | Badger closes the book for good
test: ★★ The Queen's «Off with her head!» and Josh's sister's «touch my straighteners and I'll KILL you». What do they have in common? | Both are big threats made in a temper that aren't real plans | Both are real plans that will happen the next day | Both are understatement that makes things smaller | Both are polite requests said in a loud voice
test: ★★ The Rat's «a little wet» and a British dad's «slight problem in the kitchen». Which kind of talk do both use? | Understatement | Hyperbole | Sarcasm | A plain true fact
test: ★★★ Alice is «more than nine feet high». Her pool of tears is «about four inches deep». A child of 4 feet stands in it. About how far up her legs does it come? | About four inches, just over her ankles | Up to her chin, so she has to swim | Over her head, like a deep pool | About nine feet, as high as Alice
test: ★★★ Toad's song says the clever men at Oxford «none of them know one half as much» as Toad. If that were true and Toad knew 100 facts, what is the most any Oxford man could know? | Less than 50 facts | More than 200 facts | Exactly 100 facts | All the facts there are
test: ★★★ Which of these is Carroll making a saying come TRUE, rather than a character exaggerating? | The pool from Alice's gallons of tears | The Queen's «Off with her head!» | The Rabbit's «how late it's getting!» | Toad's «Finest house on the whole river»
exam: Identify the device by which Lewis Carroll represents Alice, weeping, as producing «a large pool all round her». | A figure of speech rendered literally for comic effect | A precise account of the volume of human tears | A sarcastic remark of the White Rabbit | An understatement of Alice's distress
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Toad's assertion that Toad Hall is the «Finest house on the whole river»? | A boast consistent with his habitual vanity | A measured comparison of the houses on the river | An instance of polite understatement | A sarcastic criticism of his own house
exam: Select the response consistent with the account of the Gryphon in respect of the Queen's repeated command «Off with her head!». | The executions are her fancy, and none is carried out | The executions are carried out once a minute | The executions are deferred until the trial | The executions are confined to the gardeners
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the Water Rat's question «What's a little wet to a Water Rat?», addressed to the Mole after the upsetting of the boat. | To diminish the misfortune kindly, that the Mole may be comforted | To reproach the Mole severely for his carelessness | To boast of his own powers of swimming | To complain of the coldness of the river
